{"date": "2026-10-13", "post_id": 1481224, "title": "Desert Stairway to Heaven", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-13-desert-stairway-to-heaven/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation: </strong>This stairway in Chile brings you only a little bit closer to heaven. But ooh, the sky above it <a href=\"https://youtu.be/Ly6ZhQVnVow\">makes you wonder</a>. For example, you may wonder about the dark lane of our own <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-milky-way-galaxy/\">Milky Way galaxy</a> rising behind the expansive <a href=\"https://youtu.be/o5JfmFSBDgE\">Atacama Desert</a> in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chile\">Chile</a>, the band of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-september-11-zodiacal-light-before-dawn/\">zodiacal light</a> on the upper left, the green and red <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2019-february-3-an-airglow-fan-from-lake-to-sky/\">airglow</a> just above the horizon, the star <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spica\">Spica</a> in the zodiacal light stripe, and, at the very top on the right, the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2010-september-3-the-small-cloud-of-magellan/\">Small Magellanic Cloud</a> galaxy. The Desert Staircase sculpture is in the foreground, and this image was timed to catch the picturesque peering perch just in front of the near-<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-august-15-perseid-rain/\">vertical Milky Way band</a>. Upon <a href=\"https://static.boredpanda.com/blog/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/funny-dog-nose-closeups-30__700.jpg\">close inspection</a> of the horizon you can find, left to right, some <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andes\">Andes</a> Mountains, the <a href=\"https://youtu.be/-snTWaT1RVk\">Licancabur volcano</a>, and the town lights of <a href=\"https://youtu.be/3xvnQitpPeU\">San Pedro de Atacama</a>. The <a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/julienlooten/55575540456/\">featured wide-angle composite</a> was captured in July 2025.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>open space", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/julienlooten/\">Julien Looten</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/julienlooten/\">Julien Looten</a>", "alt": "The Milky Way galaxy rises from the horizon and  curves upward in the sky. In the foreground, a rock pathway is seen that culminates in a staircase that ends abruptly. The Milky Way band appears to be a continuation of the  staircase.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-13-desert-stairway-to-heaven/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=6750&h=9000&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 13 - Desert Stairway to Heaven</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-13-desert-stairway-to-heaven/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: This stairway in Chile brings you only a little bit closer to heaven. But ooh, the sky above it makes you wonder. For example, you may wonder about the dark lane of our own Milky Way galaxy rising behind the expansive Atacama Desert in Chile, the band of zodiacal light on the upper left, the green and red airglow just above the horizon, the star Spica in the zodiacal light stripe, and, at the very top on the right, the Small Magellanic Cloud galaxy. The Desert Staircase sculpture is in the foreground, and this image was timed to catch the picturesque peering perch just in front of the near-vertical Milky Way band. Upon close inspection of the horizon you can find, left to right, some Andes Mountains, the Licancabur volcano, and the town lights of San Pedro de Atacama. The featured wide-angle composite was captured in July 2025.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: open space\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"desert stairway to heaven\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Desert Stairway to Heaven\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-13-desert-stairway-to-heaven/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=6750&#038;h=9000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=6750&#038;h=9000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"The Milky Way galaxy rises from the horizon and  curves upward in the sky. In the foreground, a rock pathway is seen that culminates in a staircase that ends abruptly. The Milky Way band appears to be a continuation of the  staircase.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-13T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-12T09:57:22-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Desert Stairway to Heaven\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=6750&#038;h=9000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 13<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg\" onMouseOver=\"if (document.images) document.imagename1.src='https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_960_annotated.jpg';\"\nonMouseOut=\"if (document.images) document.imagename1.src='https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg';\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg\" name=imagename1 alt=\"The Milky Way galaxy rises from the horizon and  curves upward in the sky. In the foreground, a rock pathway is seen that culminates in a staircase that ends abruptly. The Milky Way band appears to be a continuation of the  staircase.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Desert Stairway to Heaven </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/julienlooten/\">Julien Looten</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation: </strong>This stairway in Chile brings you only a little bit closer to heaven. But ooh, the sky above it <a href=\"https://youtu.be/Ly6ZhQVnVow\">makes you wonder</a>. For example, you may wonder about the dark lane of our own <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-milky-way-galaxy/\">Milky Way galaxy</a> rising behind the expansive <a href=\"https://youtu.be/o5JfmFSBDgE\">Atacama Desert</a> in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chile\">Chile</a>, the band of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-september-11-zodiacal-light-before-dawn/\">zodiacal light</a> on the upper left, the green and red <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2019-february-3-an-airglow-fan-from-lake-to-sky/\">airglow</a> just above the horizon, the star <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spica\">Spica</a> in the zodiacal light stripe, and, at the very top on the right, the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2010-september-3-the-small-cloud-of-magellan/\">Small Magellanic Cloud</a> galaxy. The Desert Staircase sculpture is in the foreground, and this image was timed to catch the picturesque peering perch just in front of the near-<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-august-15-perseid-rain/\">vertical Milky Way band</a>. Upon <a href=\"https://static.boredpanda.com/blog/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/funny-dog-nose-closeups-30__700.jpg\">close inspection</a> of the horizon you can find, left to right, some <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andes\">Andes</a> Mountains, the <a href=\"https://youtu.be/-snTWaT1RVk\">Licancabur volcano</a>, and the town lights of <a href=\"https://youtu.be/3xvnQitpPeU\">San Pedro de Atacama</a>. The <a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/julienlooten/55575540456/\">featured wide-angle composite</a> was captured in July 2025.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>open space\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-12-jupiter-and-the-moons/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261013/html"}